 to the right of the country. That fundamentals picture points to a modest Republican advantage.\n\nThere are some signs of Democratic life: prediction markets favor Mary Peltola in the 2026 Senate race, and national coverage has framed Alaska as a potential swing state, suggesting its unique electorate — sizable Native and independent blocs plus ranked-choice voting — can diverge from the national map. But those signals come from a midterm context, and with no direct polling of the presidential race, the forecast stays close to the state's underlying lean: a Republican edge of about six points, with uncertainty wide enough to keep a Democratic upset plausible.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2436, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3013, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2918, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3121, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2994, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.311, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2901, "margin": -6.0}]}